<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bolghar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bolgar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bolghar (Bolgar) was a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, south of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatarstan">Tatarstan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and a capital and trading centre of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> from the 10th century. After the Mongol conquest it became an important city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, where coins were minted, and it flourished with stone mosques and mausoleums in the 14th century. Its ruins are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bolghar.jpg" alt="Bolghar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Indeikin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%D0%91%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B3%D0%B0%D1%80%D1%81%D0%BA%D0%BE%D0%B5_%D0%B3%D0%BE%D1%80%D0%BE%D0%B4%D0%B8%D1%89%D0%B5_9.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bolghar stood on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> about 30 km downstream from its confluence with the Kama, some 130 km from modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, in what is now Spassky District. A small modern town west of it has been called Bolgar since 1991. The city was founded in the 10th century and was the capital of Volga Bulgaria, though Russian incursions and internal fighting forced the Volga Bulgar rulers, called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagans</a>, to move their capital intermittently to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilyar">Bilyar</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> conquered Volga Bulgaria in the 13th century. A Russian annalist recorded that in the autumn of 1236 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> sacked Bolgar, killed its people and burned the city. Once <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilyar">Bilyar</a> was destroyed, the Mongol policy against rebuilding capitals meant it did not recover its status. The Mongols ended the existing political structure of the Volga Bolgars, and the realm became decentralised; the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>, known as Bolgar-al-Jadid, the new Bolgar, grew in prominence. Tax from regions such as Bolgar, Khwarizm, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> filled the coffers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, and the Mongols replaced the rulers of Bolgar and Khwarizm with their own.</p><p>The late 14th century brought decline. Bulat-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur</a> sacked the city in 1361, and in the Muscovite–Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> war of 1376 Muscovy and Nizhny Novgorod-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> briefly captured it and installed their own customs collector before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> retook it. It was also endangered by Timur during his war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>. Bolgar remained a Muslim religious centre until the middle of the 16th century, when Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a> and brought it into the Russian state. Under tsarist rule Russian commoners settled the site, and Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> issued a ukase to preserve the surviving ruins, the first Russian law aimed at protecting historical heritage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Bolgar Historical and Archaeological Complex, the ancient hill fort, was inscribed on the World Heritage List in 2014. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> call the medieval capital Shahri Bolghar, Persian for the City of Bolghar, and see it as part of their cultural heritage, since Volga Bulgaria was the predecessor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan</a>. Many regard it as an ancient and religious capital alongside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. In Soviet times Muslims who could not make the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hajj">hajj</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> travelled to Bolgar instead, in what was known as the Little Hajj.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bolghar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruined city of <b>Bolghar</b> became a religious and educational centre and a place of pilgrimage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…with honey, wax, hides and slaves, were brought from the northern peoples whom Arab writers called the Wisu and the Yura, and from the Rus, and were exchanged at <b>Bolghar</b> for silver coins, textiles, glass and other goods from Khwarazm, the Samanid lands of Central Asia and Iran.</blockquote><a href="/empire/volga-bulgaria">Volga Bulgaria · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
